  <prompt>
    Author the through-the-wire `WebApplicationFactory` seam slices under `tests/integration/seam/Compose/`
    (create the directory if absent) proving the Phase-5 concurrency + import behaviors end-to-end:
    (1) a STALE-BASE save RE-ANCHORS without an eTag 500 (task 050) — AUTO applies, REVIEW/ORPHAN surface;
    (2) CREATE-ON-SAVE then a content write does NOT throw the eTag mismatch (task 051), and a concurrent
        external edit yields a clean 412/ProblemDetails, not a 500;
    (3) an SPE HTTP 423 (Office lock) surfaces a user-actionable ProblemDetails, not a 500 (task 052), while
        a non-lock 5xx still surfaces as a generic ProblemDetails;
    (4) imported revisions/comments are accept/reject-able and SURVIVE a save (task 053), and an imported
        revision on an unresolvable paraId surfaces (not dropped).

    These are through-the-wire slices (real request pipeline via `WebApplicationFactory`) per ADR-038 / NFR-06.
    NO `Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;`, NO DI-registration tests, NO ctor-null tests. Cover the NEGATIVE cases
    (un-re-anchorable ORPHAN, non-lock 5xx, unresolvable imported anchor) explicitly.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Senior .NET 8 test engineer fluent in `WebApplicationFactory` integration/seam testing of a Minimal API
    save/load pipeline. Understands the ADR-038 seam DoD: a through-the-wire slice is the definition of done
    for every save/load/dispatch change, and the banned patterns (mock handler / DI-registration / ctor-null).
  </role>

  <goal>
    A green `tests/integration/seam/Compose/` suite proving all four Phase-5 behaviors through the real request
    pipeline: stale-base save re-anchors (no eTag 500); create-on-save then content write does not throw (and
    concurrent-edit yields 412); HTTP 423 surfaces a ProblemDetails (non-lock 5xx stays generic); imported
    revisions/comments are accept/reject-able and survive save (unresolvable anchor surfaces). Negative cases
    covered.
  </goal>

